      "score": 3,
      "rationale": "GPU compute (~95 H100-hours in HIL mode) and model availability (Pythia-1B, OLMo-2-0425-1B) are confirmed feasible within both hard constraints; however, the zero-shot cross-architecture transfer claim has a concrete methodological flaw (LIA attention weights α are learned over Pythia layer indices and activation dimensions — they cannot be applied directly to OLMo without explicit alignment, since the two models have different internal geometries despite matching layer counts), and LWSP's sensitivity discriminability at 1B scale is an unvalidated assumption that the fallback plan addresses incompletely."
    },
    "clarity": {
      "score": 3,
      "rationale": "The Round 2 CRITICAL issue — 'unpublished mechanistic work (pending arXiv)' cited as primary support for the activation-space orthogonality hypothesis — persists verbatim in Round 3 without revision; additionally, the probe training data size is still ambiguous: the LIA description uses 'pool(δᴸ)' implying the 300 inputs are pooled into a single checkpoint-level vector before the probe, leaving effectively 20 training examples rather than the 6,000 the Round 2 reviewer requested."
    },
    "impact": {
      "score": 4,
      "rationale": "Stage attribution is a genuinely actionable capability (SFT contamination requires retraining the instruction stage; RL contamination requires reward model decontamination; data-refresh requires corpus curation), and the EMNLP 2026 special theme alignment on data governance is strong; real-world utility is contingent on 1B-scale detection reliability, which is the right scale to study given the No Memorization No Detection findings."
    }
  },
  "issues": [
    "CRITICAL (persisted from Round 2, unresolved): The proposal still reads 'Unpublished mechanistic work (pending arXiv) shows SFT and RL probe directions are nearly orthogonal in activation space (cosine ≈ −0.07), directly supporting the hypothesis...' Round 2 explicitly labeled this CRITICAL and required converting it into SCAF's own empirical finding (a cosine-similarity heatmap of per-stage LIA directions as Figure 1 or an analysis section). Submitting with an unverifiable pending-arXiv citation will draw an instant desk reject or reviewer rejection — this is a non-negotiable fix.",
    "MODERATE (persisted from Round 2, not demonstrably resolved): The probe classifier data size issue remains unresolved in the description. LIA says 'a soft attention α = softmax(Wα · pool(δᴸ)) is learned over all layers' — the 'pool(δᴸ)' phrase implies the 300 per-input activation differences are pooled per checkpoint before the attention step, leaving 20 checkpoint-level vectors as the probe training set. LWSP is unambiguously checkpoint-level (one L-dimensional vector per checkpoint). Round 2 requested the majority-voting fix: treat each of the 300 inputs as a separate probe training instance with its checkpoint's stage label, then aggregate predictions per checkpoint at test time. The proposal does not state this fix was implemented.",
    "MODERATE (new): Zero-shot cross-architecture LIA transfer is methodologically fragile. The LIA attention weights α are trained on Pythia-1B activation-difference vectors; these weights encode which *Pythia* layers respond most to contamination. OLMo-2-0425-1B has different internal representation geometry even if layer counts match (different normalization: OLMo-2 uses QK-norm and different attention parameterization vs. Pythia's GPT-NeoX style). Applying Pythia's α directly to OLMo activation differences without normalization or whitening is equivalent to assuming the two models' layer representations live in the same space — a strong unsupported assumption. The LWSP feature is actually architecture-agnostic (scalar per block) and is the stronger candidate for zero-shot transfer. Fix: either (a) apply a learned per-architecture scaling/whitening to align the activation spaces before LIA, or (b) separately ablate LIA-only vs. LWSP-only transfer to show which component actually generalizes.",
    "MINOR (new): The LWSP layer distribution hypothesis — 'data-refresh contamination distributes sensitivity broadly across early layers; SFT concentrates it in middle layers; RL shifts it toward later layers' — is presented as established fact used to motivate LWSP, but is itself an untested prediction. If 100M-token continued pretraining produces diffuse weight changes indistinguishable from clean training at Pythia-1B scale (plausible given How Much Can We Forget's finding that contamination fades after 5× Chinchilla data), LWSP will fail to identify the data-refresh condition. The day-2 early ablation checkpoint mitigates this but the fallback plan ('use LIA + CoDeC-style ICL sensitivity') is too vaguely specified to be credible to reviewers — the fallback should be pre-registered as a concrete alternative table in the paper.",
    "MINOR: The venue priority logic remains suboptimal. EMNLP 2026 May 25 deadline is 16 days from today; HIL mode requires 7-10 days of experiments, leaving 6-9 days for writing, submission infrastructure, and author sign-off. This is achievable only if a draft exists before experiments begin. The proposal should state clearly that AAAI 2027 (July-August 2026 deadline) is the primary target and EMNLP 2026 is a stretch target contingent on pre-existing paper draft, not present it as roughly co-equal."
  ],
  "feedback": "The core architecture of this paper is sound and the gap is real — my independent searches across the awesome-data-contamination repo, ICLR 2026 proceedings, and arXiv 2026 confirm no paper attempts stage attribution. Three fixes are required before this can pass: (1) **Eliminate the pending-arXiv citation immediately.** Add a short analysis section titled 'Do Training Stages Leave Distinguishable Activation Signatures?' that computes cosine similarity between per-stage LIA direction vectors as an empirical contribution of SCAF itself. This converts an unverifiable citation into a first result supporting the method — which is stronger, not weaker, than citing an external unpublished paper. (2) **Explicitly state the majority-voting probe design.** Replace the ambiguous 'pool(δᴸ)' description with explicit language: the 300 activation-difference vectors per checkpoint are used as individual training instances (class label = checkpoint's stage), and at test time, per-input predictions are aggregated by majority vote per checkpoint. Make clear that LWSP vectors are concatenated per input (the same LWSP for all 300 inputs from a checkpoint), and that the probe trains on 20×300 = 6,000 instances total. (3) **Decouple LIA and LWSP in the cross-architecture evaluation.** Table 3 (cross-arch zero-shot) should have separate rows for LWSP-only transfer and LIA+LWSP transfer, with a note explaining why LWSP is expected to generalize better. If LIA transfer requires whitening, implement and report it. These three fixes are all implementable in under 48 hours and bring the paper to submission-ready state for AAAI 2027; EMNLP 2026 is achievable only with an aggressive writing schedule starting immediately alongside experiments.",
